# Solution — GIAM 6.5 Problem 3

## Problem

The natural logarithm function $ \ln(x) $ is defined by the definite integral

$$$
\ln(x) \;=\; \int_{t=1}^{x} \frac{1}{t}\, dt, \qquad x > 0.
$$$

Show that $ \ln(x) $ is strictly increasing on its entire domain $ (0, \infty) $.

## Setup

A function $ f $ defined on a set $ D $ is **strictly increasing** if

$$$
a, b \in D, \;\; a < b \;\Longrightarrow\; f(a) < f(b).
$$$

So we must show: for every pair $ 0 < a < b $, $ \ln(a) < \ln(b) $.

The integrand $ 1/t $ is continuous and strictly positive on all of $ (0, \infty) $. That single fact will drive the entire proof.

## The Argument

Geometrically, $ \ln(b) - \ln(a) $ is the area under the curve $ y = 1/t $ between $ t = a $ and $ t = b $. Since the integrand is strictly positive throughout $ (0, \infty) $, that strip has positive area whenever $ a < b $ — and the algebraic argument below just makes this rigorous.

!(70%)[Area under y = 1/t between t=a and t=b](problem-3-graph.svg "The integrand y = 1/t is positive on (0, ∞); the shaded strip from t = a to t = b has area ln(b) − ln(a) > 0.")

Fix $ 0 < a < b $. By additivity of the integral over adjacent intervals,

$$$
\int_{1}^{b} \frac{1}{t}\, dt \;=\; \int_{1}^{a} \frac{1}{t}\, dt \;+\; \int_{a}^{b} \frac{1}{t}\, dt,
$$$

so the difference of the two logarithms is itself a definite integral:

$$$
\ln(b) - \ln(a) \;=\; \int_{1}^{b} \frac{1}{t}\, dt \;-\; \int_{1}^{a} \frac{1}{t}\, dt \;=\; \int_{a}^{b} \frac{1}{t}\, dt.
$$$

(The identity holds regardless of whether $ a, b $ lie above or below $ 1 $; the convention $ \int_{p}^{q} = -\int_{q}^{p} $ keeps the bookkeeping consistent.)

Now bound the integrand from below. For every $ t \in [a, b] $,

$ 0 \;<\; a \;\leq\; t \;\leq\; b, $

and reciprocating (which reverses inequalities for positive numbers) gives

$ \frac{1}{t} \;\geq\; \frac{1}{b} \;>\; 0. $

So the integrand exceeds the positive constant $ 1/b $ throughout $ [a, b] $, and the integral is correspondingly bounded:

$$$
\int_{a}^{b} \frac{1}{t}\, dt \;\geq\; \int_{a}^{b} \frac{1}{b}\, dt \;=\; \frac{b - a}{b} \;>\; 0.
$$$

Therefore

$ \ln(b) - \ln(a) \;\geq\; \frac{b - a}{b} \;>\; 0, $

i.e., $ \ln(a) < \ln(b) $. Since this holds for every $ 0 < a < b $, $ \ln $ is strictly increasing on $ (0, \infty) $. $ \qquad \blacksquare $

## Remark — The Calculus View

The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus gives an alternative, even shorter proof. Since $ 1/t $ is continuous on $ (0, \infty) $, the FTC says

$$$
\frac{d}{dx} \ln(x) \;=\; \frac{1}{x}.
$$$

For every $ x > 0 $, $ 1/x > 0 $, so the derivative of $ \ln $ is strictly positive on its entire domain. Any function with strictly positive derivative on an interval is strictly increasing there — so $ \ln $ is strictly increasing on $ (0, \infty) $.

Both arguments rest on the same underlying fact: $ 1/t > 0 $ for every $ t > 0 $. The integral-difference proof above extracts this directly from the definition of $ \ln $; the FTC proof routes it through differentiation. Either way, the strict positivity of the integrand on $ (0, \infty) $ is the engine behind the strict monotonicity of $ \ln $.
